---

<Base
  title="gm docs — reference"
  description="Reference for gm: every subcommand and flag, finder keys and slash commands, gm.toml settings, ranking, files and environment variables."
  current="docs"
>
  <main class="container docs">
    <aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="toc-title">Reference</p>
      <ol>
        {
          toc.map(([id, name, sub]) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`#${id}`}>{name}</a>
              {sub.length > 0 && (
                <ol>
                  {sub.map(([sid, sname]) => (
                    <li>
                      <a href={`#${sid}`}>{sname}</a>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ol>
              )}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </aside>

    <article class="doc">
      <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
      <h1>Reference</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        What every command, flag, key and setting does, as the source implements it. For a first install, see
        <a href={`${base}#install`}>Install</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="cli">Command line</h2>

      <h3 id="synopsis">Synopsis</h3>
      <Code code={synopsis} />

      <h3 id="flags">Flag syntax</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Flags come before the arguments. The first argument that is not a flag ends them, and so does <code>--</code>.</li>
        <li><code>-name</code> and <code>--name</code> are the same flag. A value follows as the next argument or after <code>=</code>: <code>-b main</code>, <code>--branch=main</code>.</li>
        <li>A switch also takes <code>=true</code> / <code>=false</code> (<code>1 t T TRUE True</code>, <code>0 f F FALSE False</code>).</li>
        <li><code>-h</code> or <code>--help</code> after a subcommand prints its flags and exits 0. An unknown flag prints <code>flag provided but not defined: -x</code> with the flags and exits 2.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="exit">Exit status and output</h3>
      <RefTable
        head={["Status", "When"]}
        rows={[
          ["`0`", "Success, including quitting the finder and answering `n` to a prompt"],
          ["`1`", "An error. It is printed on stderr as `gm: <message>`"],
          ["`2`", "Wrong usage: an unknown subcommand prints the usage, an unknown flag the flags"],
        ]}
      />
      <p>
        Paths a script needs go to stdout: the finder's choice, <code>gm create</code>, <code>gm wt create</code>,
        <code>gm migrate</code>. Progress, warnings and prompts go to stderr. Every prompt reads <code>[y/N]</code>: only
        <code>y</code> or <code>yes</code> (any case) goes ahead, and a closed stdin answers no. <code>gm help</code> and
        <code>gm version</code> work even when <code>gm.toml</code> cannot be read.
      </p>

      <h2 id="commands">Subcommands</h2>

      <h3 id="cmd-gm">gm</h3>
      <p>
        Opens the <a href="#finder">finder</a> on the repositories under every root and prints the chosen path. Quitting
        prints nothing. A visit is recorded for the chosen repository or worktree. With no repository under any root it
        fails with <code>no repositories under &lt;root&gt;; try `gm get &lt;repo&gt;`</code>.
      </p>

      <h3 id="cmd-get">gm get <code>[flags] &lt;repo&gt;...</code></h3>
      <p>
        Clones each <a href="#refs"><code>&lt;repo&gt;</code></a> to <code>&lt;primary root&gt;/host/user/repo</code>, with
        submodules. A repository already cloned under exactly one root is that one; under two or more roots it is an error
        naming them, since gm will not pick one. Alias <code>gm clone</code>.
      </p>
      <RefTable head={["Flag", "Effect"]} rows={getFlags} />
      <RefTable head={["stderr line", "Meaning"]} rows={getMessages} />
      <p>
        With <code>-u</code>, a detached <code>HEAD</code> or a branch without an upstream is fetched but not moved, and
        says nothing. After a fast-forward, submodules are updated with <code>git submodule update --init --recursive</code>
        when <code>.gitmodules</code> exists and <code>--no-recursive</code> was not given.
      </p>

      <h3 id="cmd-list">gm list <code>[flags] [&lt;query&gt;]</code></h3>
      <p>
        Prints the repositories as <code>host/user/repo</code>, sorted. Several query words are joined with a space. Without
        <code>-e</code>, a repository matches when its <code>host/user/repo</code> contains the query (case-sensitive).
        Alias <code>gm ls</code>.
      </p>
      <RefTable head={["Flag", "Effect"]} rows={listFlags} />

      <h3 id="cmd-status">gm status <code>[flags]</code></h3>
      <p>
        Lists unfinished work in every repository and every worktree under <code>&lt;root&gt;/.worktrees</code>: a row is
        unfinished when it has uncommitted changes or unpushed commits. It never fetches, so it is fast and works offline;
        <code>behind</code> is as old as the last fetch. Each line is the name, padded, then a summary.
      </p>
      <RefTable head={["Flag", "Effect"]} rows={statusFlags} />
      <RefTable head={["Summary word", "Meaning"]} rows={statusWords} />

      <h3 id="cmd-create">gm create <code>[--ssh] &lt;repo&gt;</code></h3>
      <p>
        Makes the directory under the primary root, runs <code>git init</code> and <code>git remote add origin
        &lt;url&gt;</code>, then prints the path. <code>--ssh</code> sets <code>origin</code> to the SSH URL. Refused when the
        repository exists under any root or the directory is not empty; if a step fails, what was made is removed. Alias
        <code>gm new</code>.
      </p>

      <h3 id="cmd-remove">gm remove <code>[--dry-run] [-y] &lt;repo&gt;...</code></h3>
      <p>
        Removes each repository, every worktree checked out of it, and the empty directories left above them. Alias
        <code>gm rm</code>. A query first matches exactly (name, <code>user/repo</code>, <code>host/user/repo</code> or the
        path), else by substring; more than one hit is an error listing them.
      </p>
      <RefTable head={["Flag", "Effect"]} rows={dryYes} />
      <p>
        Before asking <code>remove &lt;path&gt;?</code> it warns about uncommitted changes in the repository, names the
        worktrees that go with it and the changed files in each (they are removed with <code>--force</code>), and names
        anything else in its worktree directory that would go too.
      </p>

      <h3 id="cmd-wt">gm wt <code>create | remove | expire</code></h3>
      <p>
        The finder's worktree actions for scripts. A worktree of <code>&lt;repo&gt;</code> for <code>&lt;branch&gt;</code>
        lives at <code>&lt;root&gt;/.worktrees/host/user/repo/&lt;branch&gt;</code>.
      </p>
      <RefTable
        head={["Command", "Flags", "Effect"]}
        rows={[
          ["`gm wt create <repo> <branch>` (alias `new`)", "none", "Check the branch out at that path, starting it from `HEAD` if it does not exist. Refused if the path exists. stderr: `created  <repo> (new branch|existing branch)`; stdout: the path"],
          ["`gm wt remove <repo> <branch>` (alias `rm`)", "`--dry-run` `-y`", "Remove that worktree (never the main one), warning about uncommitted changes first, then delete its branch if `git branch -d` allows: `deleted  branch <b>` or `kept     (not merged) branch <b>`"],
          ["`gm wt expire <repo> <days>d`", "`--dry-run` `-y`", "Remove every worktree whose `HEAD` has not moved for that many days (by its reflog), and each one's merged branch. One with uncommitted changes is kept (`keep ... (uncommitted changes)`); so are the main worktree and one without a reflog. `<days>` is a positive integer: `30d`"],
        ]}
      />

      <h3 id="cmd-migrate">gm migrate <code>[--dry-run] [-y] [-r] &lt;directory&gt;...</code></h3>
      <p>
        Moves existing clones into the tree, to the path their <code>origin</code> remote says, asking
        <code>move &lt;src&gt; -&gt; &lt;dst&gt;?</code> for each. Worktrees of a moved repository are repaired; those kept
        inside its directory move with it. A directory named on the command line that cannot move is an error; one found by
        <code>-r</code> is skipped.
      </p>
      <RefTable head={["Flag", "Effect"]} rows={migrateFlags} />
      <RefTable head={["Output", "Meaning"]} rows={migrateOutcomes} />

      <h3 id="cmd-root">gm root <code>[--all]</code></h3>
      <p>Prints the primary root, the one new repositories go to. <code>--all</code> prints every root, one per line, in order.</p>

      <h3 id="cmd-shell">gm shell <code>&lt;fish|zsh|bash&gt;</code></h3>
      <p>
        Prints the key binding for <code>launch_key</code> (default <code>Ctrl-G</code>): it runs <code>gm</code> and
        <code>cd</code>s to the printed path. In fish it is bound in insert mode too, for vi key bindings.
      </p>
      <RefTable
        head={["Shell", "Add to"]}
        rows={[
          ["fish", "`gm shell fish | source` in `~/.config/fish/config.fish`"],
          ["zsh", "`eval \"$(gm shell zsh)\"` in `~/.zshrc`"],
          ["bash", "`eval \"$(gm shell bash)\"` in `~/.bashrc`"],
        ]}
      />
      <p>After changing <code>launch_key</code>, re-run the line or restart the shell.</p>

      <h3 id="cmd-version">gm version / gm help</h3>
      <p>
        <code>gm version</code> (<code>-v</code>, <code>--version</code>) prints <code>gm &lt;version&gt;</code>; a build
        without a release version says <code>dev</code>. <code>gm help</code> (<code>-h</code>, <code>--help</code>) prints the
        usage.
      </p>

      <h2 id="refs">Repository references</h2>
      <p>
        Wherever a command takes <code>&lt;repo&gt;</code> to clone or create, it is turned into a URL, and the URL's
        <code>host/path</code>, without <code>.git</code>, is the directory under the root.
      </p>
      <RefTable head={["Written as", "URL", "Directory"]} rows={refs} />
      <ul>
        <li><code>&lt;you&gt;</code> is <code>git config github.user</code>, else <code>$USER</code>.</li>
        <li>A host needs a dot and a top-level label of letters, optionally a port: <code>example.com</code>, <code>git.example.com:8443</code>.</li>
        <li><code>--ssh</code> turns an <code>https</code> URL into <code>ssh://git@host/...</code>.</li>
        <li>Refused: an empty reference, a <code>.</code> or <code>..</code> path segment, and a space or control character anywhere in the host or path.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Commands that act on a repository already cloned (<code>remove</code>, <code>wt</code>) take a query instead, matched
        as in <a href="#cmd-remove"><code>gm remove</code></a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="layout">Directory layout</h2>
      <Code
        code={`~/ghq/github.com/jedipunkz/gm/                            repository
~/ghq/.worktrees/github.com/jedipunkz/gm/feat/login       worktree of branch feat/login
~/ghq/.worktrees/github.com/jedipunkz/gm/.forks/bob/main  worktree of a pull request from bob's fork`}
      />
      <ul>
        <li>A repository is a directory holding <code>.git</code>, <code>.hg</code> or <code>.svn</code>. The walk never descends into a repository or into a directory whose name starts with a dot.</li>
        <li>So <code>.worktrees</code> is never listed as repositories, and does not get in the way of <code>gm migrate</code>.</li>
        <li>A pull request from the same repository is filed under its head branch. One from a fork goes under <code>.forks/&lt;owner&gt;/</code>: no branch name has a component starting with a dot, so a fork's <code>main</code> cannot collide with <code>main</code> or <code>bob/main</code>.</li>
        <li>Mercurial and Subversion working copies are listed, but gm only clones with git.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="finder">Finder</h2>
      <p>
        The list hangs from the prompt at the bottom: the best match is the last row, next to the cursor. The details pane is
        on the right, and the line under the prompt shows the keys, or a note, or a spinner with what gm is waiting on and
        for how long.
      </p>

      <h3 id="lists">Lists</h3>
      <RefTable head={["List", "Opened by", "Rows", "Enter"]} rows={lists} />
      <p>
        Every list but the repositories belongs to the repository selected when it was opened. Going back restores the
        repository list's query, cursor and highlights. <code>#42</code> in the pull request list finds number 42: the
        number leads each row. Picking a remote branch makes a local branch that tracks it, fetching it first if the last
        fetch did not bring it.
      </p>

      <h3 id="keys">Keys</h3>
      <RefTable head={["Key", "Repository list", "Other lists"]} rows={keys} />
      <p><code>Ctrl-W</code> <code>Ctrl-L</code> <code>Ctrl-J</code> <code>Ctrl-Alt-B</code> are the defaults of <a href="#chords">configurable keys</a>.</p>

      <h3 id="editing">Text editing</h3>
      <RefTable head={["Key", "Effect"]} rows={editing} />

      <h3 id="slash">Slash commands</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>A <code>/</code> at the start of an empty box types a command; elsewhere a slash keeps filtering, so <code>acme/alpha</code> is a query.</li>
        <li>To act on a search result, end the query with <code>;</code> and type the command: <code>gm;/remove</code>. Or <code>Esc</code> empties the box and keeps the selection.</li>
        <li>The rest of the command name is shown after the cursor; <code>Tab</code> accepts it. <code>Enter</code> runs the command.</li>
        <li>An unknown command, or one missing its argument, leaves a note under the prompt and does nothing.</li>
      </ul>
      <RefTable head={["Command", "Action", "Repos", "Worktrees", "Branches", "PRs"]} rows={slash} />

      <h3 id="panels">Panels</h3>
      <p>
        <code>/create</code>, <code>/remove</code> and <code>/expire</code> ask first, in a panel over the list that names
        what will happen, including uncommitted changes that would be lost. While a panel is up, every other key is ignored.
      </p>
      <RefTable head={["Panel", "Accept", "Close"]} rows={panels} />

      <h3 id="details">Details pane</h3>
      <p>Each field's label sits above its value. Git details load once the cursor rests on a row for 100 ms.</p>
      <RefTable head={["Field", "Shows"]} rows={details} />

      <h3 id="network">Network calls</h3>
      <p>
        Calls that reach a remote run with a deadline and cannot prompt: <code>GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0</code>, ssh with
        <code>BatchMode=yes</code>, <code>GH_PROMPT_DISABLED=1</code>, stdin closed. When the deadline passes, the process
        group is killed and the error says what ran and for how long. The branch list uses git's own credentials and never
        <code>gh</code>; the pull request list needs <a href="https://cli.github.com/"><code>gh</code></a>, logged in.
      </p>
      <RefTable head={["Call", "Deadline", "Notes"]} rows={network} />
      <p>
        The browser is <code>open</code> on macOS, <code>wslview</code> or <code>explorer.exe</code> on WSL, and
        <code>xdg-open</code> elsewhere. The remote is turned into an <code>https</code> URL first, without the port.
      </p>

      <h2 id="config">Configuration</h2>

      <h3 id="settings">Settings</h3>
      <p>
        <code>$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gm/gm.toml</code>, else <code>~/.config/gm/gm.toml</code>. The file is optional. A file that
        cannot be parsed, a key gm does not know, or a value of the wrong type is an error naming the file, never a setting
        half ignored. An empty string means the default.
      </p>
      <RefTable head={["Key", "Type", "Default", "Meaning"]} rows={settings} />
      <Code code={toml} label="gm.toml" />

      <h3 id="root">Root resolution</h3>
      <p>The first source that is set wins; the sources after it are not read.</p>
      <RefTable head={["#", "Source", "Notes"]} rows={rootOrder} />
      <ul>
        <li><code>~</code> and <code>~/...</code> expand to <code>$HOME</code>. Every root must then be absolute, or gm refuses to run.</li>
        <li>Duplicates are dropped. An empty <code>root</code> string or list is an error.</li>
        <li>The first root is the primary one: <code>get</code>, <code>create</code> and <code>migrate</code> put repositories there. Every root is listed and searched.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="themes">Themes</h3>
      <p>{themes.map((t, i) => <>{i > 0 && " "}<code>{t.name}</code></>)}</p>
      <p>The default is <code>tokyonight</code>. The palettes are shown on the <a href={`${base}#themes`}>home page</a>.</p>

      <h3 id="chords">Key bindings</h3>
      <p>
        A chord is Ctrl, optionally Alt and Shift in any order, and one letter. Case does not matter:
        <code>ctrl-alt-b</code>, <code>Ctrl+Alt+B</code>, <code>c-a-b</code>, <code>^meta-b</code> and
        <code>ctrl-shift-alt-b</code> are all accepted. An invalid chord is an error, never a silent no-op.
      </p>
      <RefTable head={["Part", "Spellings"]} rows={chordSyntax} />
      <RefTable head={["Setting", "Refused"]} rows={chordRules} />
      <RefTable head={["Chord", "Reaches gm"]} rows={reach} />
      <p>
        Avoid chords the shell or terminal keeps for itself: <code>ctrl-r</code> (history search), <code>ctrl-c</code>
        <code>ctrl-d</code> <code>ctrl-z</code> (signals).
      </p>

      <h2 id="ranking">Ranking</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>A repository is a candidate when its <code>host/user/repo</code> holds the query's characters in order, ignoring case.</li>
        <li>Where they matched is the literal substring if the query occurs as one; otherwise the fuzzy matcher's positions.</li>
        <li>Those positions are scored with the weights below. The highest score goes to the bottom row.</li>
        <li>Equal scores are ordered by frecency. With no query, the list is ordered by frecency alone, highest at the bottom.</li>
      </ol>
      <RefTable head={["Matched character", "Score"]} rows={weights} />
      <p>Frecency is the visit count weighted by how long ago the last visit was:</p>
      <RefTable head={["Last visit", "Frecency"]} rows={frecency} />
      <p>
        A visit is recorded when the finder chooses a row, and when <code>get</code>, <code>create</code>,
        <code>migrate</code> or <code>wt create</code> fetches, makes or moves a repository.
      </p>

      <h2 id="files">Files and environment</h2>
      <RefTable head={["File", "Purpose"]} rows={files} />
      <RefTable head={["Variable", "Effect"]} rows={envs} />
      <RefTable head={["git config", "Effect"]} rows={gitConfig} />
      <p>Requirements: macOS or Linux (WSL2 included), <code>git</code> on <code>$PATH</code>, <code>gh</code> for the pull request list, and a true-color terminal for the themes.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>

